  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">FEMIN-ACT: Feminist Solutions to Climate Change-Driven Food Insecurity in Iraq</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The project&apos;s implementers, including Action Against Hunger (ACF), Oxfam, their project teams in Iraq as well as local partners, will provide technical assistance and capacity building to agricultural producers, value chain actors, government stakeholders and community leaders in targeted communities across four governorates, who will ultimately contribute to and benefit from increased equitable and sustainable food security. 600 (180f, 420m) smallholder farmers will be supported with trainings and inputs under the project, and 3,000 (1,470f, 1,530m) community members will be directly targeted through behavior change in climate change mitigation approaches. Additionally, 120 (59f, 61m) pastoralists will be supported with livestock management services. An additional 3,540 (1,735f, 1,805m) community members will be provided capacity building and infrastructural support in water management for irrigation purposes. 210 (63f, 147m) individuals will be directly reached through awareness raising activities at the community level, on women&apos;s economic empowerment, unpaid care work, and other related barriers to women&apos;s participation in the identified agricultural value chains, and in climate governance. An estimated   9,922 (4,481f, 5,441m) community members, across the four targeted governorates, will be indirectly reached, through awareness raising campaigns via radio shows, on issues that are prohibiting women&apos;s economic empowerment, as well as awareness on climate change. An additional estimated 16,000 (7,840f,8,160m) community members will be indirectly reached with &#x2018;buy -local&apos; campaigns, to promote local products within targeted value chains.</narrative>
  </description>
